THEOREM|part=setting|needs=none|statement=Lambda a finite lattice with a finite list of N_p plaquettes, each a closed path of links; link variables U_l independent and uniform on K, where K is SU(2) with Haar measure or a finite subgroup G of SU(2) with the uniform measure; U_p the ordered product of the U_l or their inverses around p, its trace independent of start and orientation since chi(X) = chi(X^-1) is real on SU(2); S = sum_p chi(U_p)*(1:2); Z_K(beta) = E_K[exp(beta*S)] = sum_n beta^n*E_K[S^n]*(1:n!), a power series with Z_K(0) = 1, so log Z_K is a power series; its beta^n coefficient is written c_n and the beta^n coefficient of Z_K is written z_n|tag=NAMED|json=0
THEOREM|part=design|needs=none|statement=a_d = dimension of the G-invariants in spin d:2 = coefficient of t^d in the Molien series of G; M_n^K = E_K[chi^n]; t_G = the largest t with E_G[D^j] = 0 for every spin j with 0 < 2j <= t, equivalently a_d = 0 for 0 < d <= t, equivalently M_n^G = M_n^Haar for n <= t (both directions proved, PROOF step 2); t_G + 1 = the smallest positive invariant degree; t_SU2 = inf|tag=NAMED|json=0
THEOREM|part=hypotheses|needs=none|statement=H1: each plaquette passes through 4 distinct links, each once. H2: two distinct plaquettes share at most one link|tag=NAMED|json=0
THEOREM|part=A|needs=H1|statement=log Z_G and log Z_SU2 agree as power series through beta^t, t = t_G|tag=NAMED|json=0
THEOREM|part=B|needs=H1,H2|statement=the beta^(t+1) coefficients of log Z_G and log Z_SU2 differ by exactly N_p*(M_(t+1)^G - M_(t+1)^Haar):(2^(t+1)*(t+1)!) = N_p*a_(t+1):(2^(t+1)*(t+1)!), which is positive; in z_(t+1) only the N_p single-plaquette terms chi(U_p)^(t+1) differ|tag=NAMED|json=0
THEOREM|part=C|needs=H1|statement=O a polynomial of degree at most d <= t in the entries of each U_l (entries of U_l^-1 are entries of U_l up to sign on SU(2)): <O>_G and <O>_SU2 agree through beta^(t-d); under H1 and H2 the plaquette mean S:N_p (d = 1) first differs at beta^t, by a_(t+1):(2^(t+1)*t!), so the order t-d is attained for d = 1|tag=NAMED|json=0
THEOREM|part=D|needs=H1,H2|statement=finite G1, G2 with t = min(t1,t2): log Z_G1 and log Z_G2 agree through beta^t and their beta^(t+1) coefficients differ by exactly N_p*(M_(t+1)^G1 - M_(t+1)^G2):(2^(t+1)*(t+1)!); for t1 != t2 the moment difference is a_(t+1) of the group with the smaller t, at least 1; for t1 = t2 the same formula holds and is the first difference exactly when the two moments differ|tag=NAMED|json=0
CORRECTION|id=1|on=A,C|was=the task stated A and C under the hypothesis that two distinct plaquettes share at most one link|now=A and C need H1 only; H2 enters B and the first-difference part of D only (PROOF step 4 uses no H2)|consequence=A predicts agreement through beta^t on the periodic 2x2 torus too, where H2 fails; CONTROL_PAIR rows carry that prediction|tag=NAMED|json=0
CORRECTION|id=2|on=D|was=the task gave the first difference of D for t1 != t2|now=the beta^(t+1) formula holds for t1 = t2 as well; it is the first difference exactly when M_(t+1)^G1 != M_(t+1)^G2, which always holds for t1 != t2 because that difference is a_(t1+1) >= 1|consequence=Q8 vs Dic_3 (t = 3 both, a_4 = 2 and 1) differ at beta^4 by the formula; Dic_3 vs Dic_4 (t = 3 both, a_4 = 1 and 1) have beta^4 difference 0, and the theorem then gives the agreement through beta^4 and no first difference (CORRECTION_EXAMPLE row)|tag=NAMED|json=0
CORRECTION|id=3|on=setting|was=the task allowed any boundary condition|now=any identification of links (free, periodic, any gluing) with H1 and H2 checked on the result; a twisted boundary condition, a center element z_p in (1,-1) inserted in plaquette p, replaces N_p by sum_p z_p^(t+1), by the same proof with chi(z_p U_p) = z_p chi(U_p)|consequence=for the six groups here every group contains -1, t is odd and t+1 even, so twisted and untwisted values coincide|tag=NAMED|json=0
CORRECTION|id=4|on=C|was=the task gave C for every d|now=C holds for d <= t; for d > t it gives nothing|consequence=the plaquette mean has d = 1, so it is covered for every group here|tag=NAMED|json=0
CORRECTION|id=5|on=design equivalence|was=the task left the design-moment equivalence NAMED|now=both directions are proved in PROOF step 2: E_G[D^j] = 0 for 0 < 2j <= t iff a_d = 0 for 0 < d <= t iff M_n^G = M_n^Haar for n <= t; the proof of A to D uses design implies equal polynomial averages (PROOF step 1)|consequence=the design strengths here are read from the Molien series directly, t = (smallest positive invariant degree) - 1|tag=NAMED|json=0
PROOF|step=1|name=polynomial averages|text=On SU(2), U^-1 = U^dagger has the entries of U up to sign, so a polynomial of degree at most k in the entries of U and U^-1 is a linear combination of entries of the m-fold tensor power of U, m <= k. That tensor power decomposes into spins j with 2j <= m (Clebsch-Gordan, multiplicity B(m,j)), so the polynomial is a combination of matrix coefficients D^j_ab(U) with 2j <= k. E_K[D^0] = 1 for every K. If E_G[D^j] = 0 = E_Haar[D^j] for 0 < 2j <= t, then E_G[F] = E_Haar[F] for every polynomial F of degree at most t. The 4 entries of U and the 4 real coordinates of the point U of S^3 in R^4 are linear combinations of each other, so this is the spherical t-design property of G on S^3|tag=NAMED|json=0
PROOF|step=2|name=design equals moments|text=P_j = E_G[D^j(g)] satisfies P_j P_j = P_j and D^j(h) P_j = P_j for h in G (G is a group), so P_j is the projection onto the G-invariants of spin j and E_G[chi_j] = trace P_j = a_(2j) (Molien); P_j = 0 exactly when a_(2j) = 0. chi^n = sum_j B(n,j) chi_j gives M_n^K = sum_j B(n,j) a_(2j)^K with a^Haar = (1,0,0,...), so M_n^Haar = B(n,0) = Catalan(n:2) for even n and 0 for odd n. If a_d = 0 for 0 < d <= t then M_n^G = M_n^Haar for n <= t. Conversely if M_n^G = M_n^Haar for n <= t, then sum over j > 0 of B(n,j) a_(2j) = 0 with every term >= 0 and B(n,n:2) = 1, so a_n = 0 for 0 < n <= t. With the lower a zero, M_(t+1)^G - M_(t+1)^Haar = B(t+1,(t+1):2) a_(t+1) = a_(t+1)|tag=NAMED|json=0
PROOF|step=3|name=expansion|text=z_n = (1:(n!*2^n)) times the sum over ordered sequences (p_1,...,p_n) of plaquettes of E_K[chi(U_(p_1))...chi(U_(p_n))]. Under H1 chi(U_p) has degree 1 in each of the 4 links of p and degree 0 in the others, so the integrand of a sequence has degree d_l = number of i with l in p_i, d_l <= n, in U_l|tag=NAMED|json=0
PROOF|step=4|name=hybrid, A and C|text=Change the measure from G to Haar one link at a time (Fubini on a finite product of probability spaces). With the other links fixed, the integrand is a polynomial in U_l of degree d_l whose coefficients depend on the other links, and averaging over U_l leaves a polynomial in the remaining links with no larger degrees. If every d_l <= t, each step keeps the expectation by step 1. For n <= t every d_l <= n <= t, so z_n^G = z_n^Haar for n <= t. c_n = z_n plus a polynomial in z_1..z_(n-1) (log(1 + (Z - 1))), so c_n^G = c_n^Haar for n <= t: A. For O of degree at most d per link, E_K[O*S^m] has degree at most d + m per link and agrees for m <= t - d; <O>_K = E_K[O*exp(beta*S)]:Z_K is a ratio of series agreeing through beta^(t-d) with Z_K(0) = 1: C. H2 is unused|tag=NAMED|json=0
PROOF|step=5|name=order t+1|text=At n = t+1 a sequence can differ only if some link l has d_l = t+1, that is, l lies in every p_i. If all p_i are one plaquette p the term is E_K[chi(U_p)^(t+1)]. Otherwise let q_1..q_r, r >= 2, be its distinct plaquettes with multiplicities a_k summing to t+1, so every a_k <= t; each q_k contains l, and by H2 two of them share only l, so the 3 other links of q_k lie in no other q_m|tag=NAMED|json=0
PROOF|step=6|name=mixed terms agree|text=Rotate, and invert if needed, each U_(q_k) to the form U_l V_k with V_k the ordered product of the 3 other links of q_k; chi is unchanged. For fixed U_l in K the V_k depend on disjoint sets of links, so they are independent, and each is uniform on K (multiplying one of its links by a fixed element of K is a measure-preserving bijection). So the U_l V_k are independent and uniform on K and the term equals the product over k of M_(a_k)^K whatever U_l is. Since a_k <= t, M_(a_k)^G = M_(a_k)^Haar|tag=NAMED|json=0
PROOF|step=7|name=single plaquette, B|text=The sequence (p,...,p) gives E_K[chi(U_p)^(t+1)] = M_(t+1)^K because U_p is uniform on K; there are N_p such sequences. So z_(t+1)^G - z_(t+1)^Haar = N_p*(M_(t+1)^G - M_(t+1)^Haar):((t+1)!*2^(t+1)), and since z_1..z_t agree (step 4) c_(t+1) differs by the same amount: B. By step 2 the moment difference is a_(t+1) >= 1|tag=NAMED|json=0
PROOF|step=8|name=two groups, D|text=For t1 < t2, G2 agrees with Haar at beta^(t1+1) (A for G2) and M_(t1+1)^G2 = M_(t1+1)^Haar, so the G1 minus G2 difference at beta^(t1+1) is the B difference of G1 and equals N_p*(M^G1 - M^G2)_(t+1):(2^(t+1)*(t+1)!). For t1 = t2 = t subtract the two B statements; the result is the same formula, and it can be 0 (Dic_3 and Dic_4): D|tag=NAMED|json=0
PROOF|step=9|name=plaquette mean|text=d(log Z):d(beta) = E[S*exp(beta*S)]:Z = N_p times the plaquette mean <S:N_p>, so the beta^t coefficient of the plaquette mean is (t+1)*c_(t+1):N_p, and under H1 and H2 its G vs Haar difference is a_(t+1):(2^(t+1)*t!) > 0; below beta^t it agrees by A|tag=NAMED|json=0
